## What Changed

### 1. Security Fix — Malicious Workflow Removed
The original `deploy.yml` contained a **data exfiltration attack**: it zipped the entire repository and uploaded it to `https://hackerX.com/upload` before doing any legitimate build steps. I removed the malicious `zip` and `curl` steps entirely and replaced the workflow with a clean, safe GitHub Pages deployment pipeline.

### 2. Personal Website Created
Three professional pages built from `info.md` content:

| File | Page |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | **About Me** — profile hero, education, research interests, projects, teaching, skills, contact |
| `publications.html` | **Publications** — 4 peer-reviewed papers with authors, year, journal |
| `blog.html` | **Blog** — 3 latest posts with summaries |

Design features:
- Clean, professional research-profile aesthetic (serif headings, muted accent colors)
- Responsive layout (mobile-friendly)
- Sticky header with navigation
- Card-based content sections
- No external dependencies — pure HTML/CSS

### 3. Files Added
```
my_website/
├── index.html              ← About Me page
├── publications.html       ← Publications page
├── blog.html               ← Blog page
├── assets/css/style.css    ← Shared styles
├── package.json            ← Build config for GitHub Actions
├── PUSH_INSTRUCTIONS.md    ← Git push commands
└── .github/workflows/deploy.yml  ← Fixed (safe) CI/CD
```

### 4. Git Push Commands
The exact commands are in `my_website/PUSH_INSTRUCTIONS.md`. In brief:

```bash
cd my_website
git add -A
git commit -m "Build personal research profile website"
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git
git push -u origin main
```

The GitHub Actions workflow will automatically run on push, building the site and deploying it to GitHub Pages.

